(vector (map (:db/id -1)
 (:adr/id "adr-2608260200-kotoba-lang-external-dependency-substitution")
 (:adr/title "Every external dependency in kotoba-lang gets a decided destination, and a detector that checks it")
 (:adr/status "accepted")
 (:adr/date "2026-08-26")
 (:adr/related (vector "adr-2607183300-kotoba-wasm-runtime-jvm-exit" "adr-2608136000-detectors-that-cannot-measure" "adr-2608260900-frontend-stack-retirement" "adr-2607173000-nbb-only-script-host"))
 (:adr/body "## Context\n\nCLAUDE.md fixes the runtime order as `kotoba wasm` > `clojurewasm` >\nClojureScript > nbb and demotes the JVM and babashka to last resort.\n`90-docs/migration/kotoba-wasm-runtime-cutover.edn` goes further and declares\n`:new-jvm-runtime-dependencies :forbidden`. Neither document says what the\ncurrent external surface actually is, so the question an author faces at the\nmoment it matters — *may I depend on this?* — had no answer that could be\nlooked up. It could only be re-derived, and it was re-derived differently\neach time.\n\n`verify-jvm-dependency-surface` answers a nearby but different question:\nWHERE a JVM is still required. That is a census of hosts. What was missing was\na census of coordinates, with a decided destination for each.\n\n## What was measured\n\n2,129 of 2,143 west-registered `orgs/kotoba-lang/` paths, on 2026-08-26. The\nwhole external surface is **26 distinct JVM runtime coordinates and 110 npm\nruntime packages** — 191 counting dev-only tooling. That is small enough to\ndecide once, per coordinate, and then check.\n\nThe measurement reshaped the work. Most of the surface is *already*\nreplaceable:\n\n- The foundational stdlib (ADR-kotoba-lang-foundational-stdlib) ships\n  `coll` `spec` `json` `wit` `async` `time` `fs` `http` `io` `test` `fmt`\n  `lsp` `scheduler` `store` `lint`, plus `net` `crypto` `tls` `kv`.\n- The origin-plane mirrors ship `org-nist-sha2` `org-ietf-blake2`\n  `org-ietf-ed25519` `org-ietf-cbor` `io-multiformats` `io-ipld-car`\n  `org-ietf-deflate` `org-ietf-x509` `org-ietf-asn1` `org-postgresql-wire`.\n- `eth-crypto` already implements secp256k1 point arithmetic and RFC 6979\n  deterministic ECDSA in portable `.cljc`, which covers most of what\n  `@noble/curves` is used for outside ed25519.\n\nSo the request *design and implement every external dependency as a library*\nresolves, for the large majority by usage, into **rewiring an import** rather\nthan writing a library. Recording that is the main value of this decision:\nwithout it, the same libraries get proposed again.\n\nFour claimed replacements did NOT survive checking, and saying so is the\npoint of measuring rather than assuming. `com-opc-ua`, `com-quic-http3` and\n`com-modbus-tcp` read like protocol implementations from their names; each is\na ~190-line CRUD scaffold over a schema. Naming them as the destination for\n`org.eclipse.milo`, `tech.kwik/kwik` and `com.digitalpetri.modbus` would have\nbeen false.\n\n## Decision\n\n**1. `manifest/dependency-substitution.edn` is the source of truth for what\nevery external dependency is supposed to become.** Each entry carries a\n`:disposition`:\n\n| disposition | meaning |\n|---|---|\n| `:structural` | the host language, its compiler, or a render backend. Not a library anyone here can write; the exit is the `.kotoba` migration |\n| `:first-party-exists` | a replacement is shipped; the work is switching the import |\n| `:gap-closed` | there was no first-party equivalent and one was written |\n| `:gap-open` | no first-party equivalent and none written; names what it would be called |\n| `:frozen-legacy` | covered by an existing cutover contract with its own exit evidence |\n| `:tooling` | dev-time only; never resolved by shipped code |\n\nAn entry may name one `:coordinate`, several under `:coordinates`, or a name\nprefix under `:pattern`. One `@radix-ui/` rule beats twenty-seven identical\nentries, and a family that grows does not silently fall out of the ledger.\nThe longest matching prefix wins, so a specific rule can still override its\nfamily.\n\n**2. `scripts/verify-dependency-substitution.cljs` enforces it.** Four\nfindings:\n\n- `unlisted-external-dependency` — a coordinate resolved by shipped code that\n  the ledger does not name. This is the finding the ledger exists to produce:\n  a new dependency that arrives without a decision.\n- `substitution-target-missing` — the ledger names a `:target` that west does\n  not register. A ledger pointing at a library nobody wrote is worse than no\n  ledger, because it reads as though the migration were already possible.\n- `builtin-shadowing-dependency` — see below.\n- `substitution-available` — replaceable today, still imported. Reported at\n  `:info`, because it is a plan and not a defect.\n\nIt refuses to report a pass it did not measure: fewer than 500 of the\nregistered paths on disk prints `SCANNED\\t0` and exits **2** — neither 0 nor\n1 — because *I could not look* and *I looked and it was clean* must not be the\nsame value (ADR-2608136000). Demonstrated: run in a worktree with no `orgs/`\ncheckouts, it exits 2 rather than reporting a clean tree.\n\n**3. Two libraries were written for gaps that had no first-party equivalent.**\n\n- **`kotoba-lang/org-ietf-websocket`** — RFC 6455 framing and handshake,\n  portable `.cljc`, zero dependencies. `inga-node` and `torihiki-node`\n  depended on npm `ws` for what is arithmetic over bytes; `ws` is a Node addon\n  boundary, so the framing vanished on any JVM or kotoba-WASM target. 20\n  tests, 117 assertions, green on both runtimes. Test vectors are RFC 6455\n  §5.7 verbatim.\n- **`kotoba-lang/org-msgpack`** — MessagePack, portable `.cljc`, zero\n  dependencies. The format already existed twice and neither copy could be\n  shared: `checkpointer` carries a JVM-only codec with throwing ClojureScript\n  stubs, and the same repository's JavaScript side depends on\n  `@msgpack/msgpack`. 16 tests, 214 assertions, green on both runtimes, with\n  20 known answers taken from the real `@msgpack/msgpack` via checkpointer's\n  own generator.\n\n## What the two-runtime rule caught\n\nBoth libraries ship a JVM runner and a ClojureScript runner, and that is not\na formality. Four defects were found that one runtime reported as green:\n\n1. **ClojureScript takes a bit-shift count mod 32.** A 64 KiB WebSocket frame\n   whose length was encoded with `>>> 48` came back as `>>> 16` and announced\n   `0x0001000000010000`. The JVM computed it correctly throughout.\n   Reintroducing the bug today still leaves `clojure -M:test` green and turns\n   the ClojureScript run red; that asymmetry was measured, not asserted.\n2. **`(map int s)` on a string.** Correct on the JVM; under ClojureScript a\n   character is a one-character string and `int` of one is not a code point,\n   so `Sec-WebSocket-Accept` was a digest of zeros.\n3. **A guard placed after the fold it was guarding.** The 64-bit length checks\n   ran after eight bytes were folded into a number — where the JVM throws\n   `long overflow` first. The guard was unreachable in precisely the case it\n   existed for. Same defect, independently, in msgpack's `read-int`, which\n   folded unsigned and subtracted `2^(8n)` afterwards.\n4. **Encoding a negative int64 as `n + 2^64`.** Overflows a JVM long before\n   the value is written. Now floored `mod`, which returns the two's-complement\n   word on both runtimes.\n\nDefect 3 is the class CLAUDE.md names in ADR-2608136000 — a check that cannot\nrun returning the same value as a check that ran and passed — appearing twice\nin one afternoon, in code written the same afternoon by someone who had just\nread that rule.\n\n## What the census found on the side\n\n`app-scap` declares `crypto`, `fs`, `net`, `path`, `tls` and `workflow` as npm\n`dependencies`, at `latest`. **These do not resolve to the Node builtins.** npm\nserves a real, unrelated package for each name: `fs@0.0.1-security` is npm's\nreclaimed-squat placeholder, `tls@0.0.1` is a stub, and `crypto`, `net` and\n`path` are abandoned userland shims. A range of `latest` means whatever is\npublished at install time. This buys nothing — `require(\"fs\")` reaches the\nbuiltin regardless — and adds six supply-chain edges. It is now its own\ndetector finding rather than a line in a table, because it is a class that\nrecurs and is invisible by inspection: the declaration looks like it is asking\nfor the builtin.\n\n`murakumo` declared `curves@^1.7.0`. The npm package `curves` is at 1.1.0 and\nunrelated, so that range **cannot resolve at all** — `npm ci` failed with\n`ETARGET` on every clean checkout of `main`.\n\n## Consequences\n\n- The question *may I depend on this?* now has a file to look in, and a new\n  dependency that arrives without a decision becomes a finding on the next\n  detector tick instead of arriving silently.\n- The remaining `:gap-open` set is small and named: Argon2 (RFC 9106),\n  BLS12-381, SQLite's file format, QUIC, OPC UA, Modbus framing, the CDP wire,\n  HPKE (RFC 9180), and TOML. Each entry says what it would be called and why\n  it has not been done. Two of them — Argon2 on top of the shipped\n  `org-ietf-blake2`, and Modbus, whose MBAP header plus PDU is small — are\n  the tractable next ones.\n- `org.xerial/sqlite-jdbc` is listed as `:gap-open` but flagged as worth\n  questioning before building. `kotobase-engine-lsm` is the first-party\n  engine; a SQLite reader adds only compatibility with files other tools\n  wrote, which may not be worth a file-format implementation.\n- Usage counts are deliberately NOT written into the ledger. They were\n  measured to decide the dispositions and they are here, in a document that\n  carries a date. A count written into a policy file becomes a constant that\n  gets quoted after it stops being true — the failure CLAUDE.md documents\n  about its own fleet-ci section. Re-measure with `--edn`.\n\n## Status of the enforcement\n\nFirst run against the real checkout, 2026-08-26: 2,131 of 2,145 registered\npaths present, 191 distinct coordinates, **0 unlisted**, **0 missing\ntargets**, 5 builtin-shadowing dependencies, 99 coordinates replaceable\ntoday. Both failure modes were shown to discriminate: pointing a `:target` at\na repository nobody wrote turns it red, and removing a coordinate the\nworkspace actually uses turns it red. Restoring returns it to the standing\nstate.\n\n## Both were fixed, and one correction to what is written above\n\n`kotoba-lang/app-scap#2` removed the six. Measured with `npm install\n--dry-run` before and after: **1159 packages → 774**, none of the six present,\n**nothing new added** (set difference computed in both directions). 385\npackages leave, most of them dragged in by `workflow@4.8.5`, which pulls\n`@vercel/*`, `@nestjs/*`, `@swc/*`, `@aws-sdk/*`, `@oclif/*`, `express`,\n`body-parser`, `undici` and `piscina` — none of which that project imports.\nAll 74 tracked files were scanned, not only the 42 source files: no reference\nto the six, nor to any of the scoped families they pulled in.\n\n`kotoba-lang/murakumo#332` fixed the `curves` line. **The instruction written\nabove — that fixing it means deleting the line — was wrong**, and\n`package-lock.json` is what proved it: the lock carried\n`\"@noble/curves\": \"^1.7.0\"` correctly the whole time, so the manifest and the\nlock had also disagreed, which is a second and independent reason `npm ci`\nrefused. The scope had been dropped from the name; the dependency is real,\nreached transitively through `kotoba-lang/witness-quorum` (which declares the\nsame `@noble/curves@^1.7.0`) when `witness_dial_attest.cljs` runs. The fix was\nrestoring the scope. Measured: `npm ci --dry-run` exit 1 before, exit 0 after.\n\nThe correction is worth keeping rather than quietly editing away, because the\nfirst diagnosis was made from `package.json` and the registry alone. The\nlockfile was sitting in the same directory and answered the question\noutright. *Two sources, one at a time* is a rule this workspace already has\nfor money; it applies to dependency manifests for exactly the same reason.\n\nAfter both merges and the west pin advances, the detector reports **0**\nbuiltin-shadowing dependencies (was 5), **0** unlisted, **0** missing targets,\n184 distinct coordinates (was 191), and 98 replaceable today.\n\n## A third gap closed, with a caveat that is part of the result\n\n`kotoba-lang/org-ietf-argon2` — RFC 9106 Argon2d, Argon2i and Argon2id in\nportable `.cljc`. This one is worth recording in more detail than the other\ntwo, because *closing the gap* and *replacing the dependency* are not the\nsame thing here and the ledger would mislead if it said only the first.\n\n**What was actually missing.** `pqh` names the suite, defines an `IKdf`\ncapability for it, and states that no Argon2id math lives there. So there was\nno in-language reference a provider could be checked against, and nothing at\nall on a host with neither BouncyCastle nor `@noble/hashes`. Both are now\nsupplied.\n\n**What is still true.** Measured at load average 66, best of three: **7.1 s on\nthe JVM and 41 s under nbb** at the OWASP minimum of 19 MiB with t=2, where a\nnative provider takes well under a tenth of a second. That is not a login\npath, and the library's README says so before it says anything else. The\ncapability seam remains the right production answer; what changed is that it\nnow has a reference implementation behind it rather than a hole.\n\nMarking this `:gap-closed` without the caveat would have been the more\nflattering entry and the false one.\n\n**How it was validated.** Three layers, because a hand-written Argon2 that\nagrees only with itself is worth nothing. RFC 9106 §5.1–5.3 verbatim, plus\ntwo vectors from `pqh` recorded there as byte-identical to `@noble/hashes` —\n**all five reproduced with BouncyCastle 1.78.1 before the implementation was\nwritten**, so the oracle existed before the code rather than being fitted to\nit. Then 150 differential comparisons against BouncyCastle across three\nvariants, nine parameter sets and five tag lengths, because five vectors\nleave untested exactly where Argon2 branches. Then both runtimes.\n\n**What the two-runtime rule caught, for the third time in this work.**\n`(mapv int \"Password\")` gives code points on the JVM and a vector of zeros\nunder ClojureScript, where a character is a one-character string — so two\ndifferent passwords hashed identically. The same defect appeared in\n`org-ietf-websocket`, where it made `Sec-WebSocket-Accept` a digest of zeros.\nTwice in two libraries by the same author on the same day. The fix here is\n`argon2.core/utf8` in the *public* API, on the reasoning that a password KDF\nwhose callers must byte-encode a string themselves is inviting it.\n\nRemaining `:gap-open`: BLS12-381, SQLite's file format, QUIC, OPC UA, Modbus\nframing, the CDP wire, HPKE, TOML.\n\n## Every gap is now decided, and four of them are decided by not building\n\nThe ledger's `:gap-open` count is **zero**. That is the state it existed to\nreach, and it is worth being precise about what reaching it meant, because\ntwo of the six dispositions record a decision rather than an implementation.\n\n**Built** (`:gap-closed`): `org-ietf-websocket`, `org-msgpack`,\n`org-ietf-argon2`, `org-modbus`, `org-toml`. Modbus is thirteen function\ncodes with MBAP, RTU and ASCII framing, tested against the specification's\nown §6 worked examples and a CRC checked three independent ways. TOML is the\nfull v1.0.0 grammar, reader only — a writer would have to answer table\nlayout, key quoting and float formatting, all policy questions a reader does\nnot, and a round-trip suite against one's own writer looks like validation\nwhile checking nothing.\n\n**Decided not to build** (`:host-boundary`), each with the reason and the\ncondition that would reopen it:\n\n- **OPC UA.** `dcs` uses Milo as a *server* — Parts 3, 4, 5 and 6 of the\n  specification. Its own `deps.edn` already states the posture: the domain\n  core is zero-dependency `.cljc` and the protocol layer is deliberately\n  host-side over an independently authored implementation. Reopens if a\n  consumer needs only the binary *encoding*.\n- **QUIC.** RFC 9000 plus 9001 plus 9002, including a TLS 1.3 integration\n  this workspace does not have either. Reopens when there is a first-party\n  TLS 1.3 handshake to build on.\n- **SQLite** — and the proposed name was wrong. Both consumers need SQL with\n  ACID transactions, not a file format: `bitcoin-consensus` validates a block\n  against an overlay on *one* transaction that commits whole or rolls back.\n  The first-party answer for the kotobase case is `kotobase-engine-lsm`,\n  which already exists, so that half is a rewire and not a library gap.\n- **BLS12-381.** Fp through Fp12, a Miller loop, a final exponentiation and\n  hash-to-curve — delicate cryptography where a subtle error is silent.\n  Reopens with a real budget; `io-filecoin-signer/testdata/gen_bls_vectors.clj`\n  means the oracle already exists, which is the expensive half.\n- **CDP and Playwright.** The wire is now buildable — it is JSON-RPC over a\n  WebSocket and `org-ietf-websocket` supplies the framing — but the wire is\n  not what Playwright provides. A first-party CDP transport with no browser\n  behind it would be a library nothing can use.\n- **The Closure Compiler and jco.** Neither is a library gap: one is a\n  compiler decision inside `amu`, the other is build tooling whose output\n  never reaches a shipped artifact.\n\n**Sequenced** (`:gap-planned`), with the blocker named rather than the size\nhidden: **HPKE** needs a first-party X25519 — HKDF is in `crypto` and\nChaCha20-Poly1305 in `noise`, but every hit for x25519 in this workspace is a\ncall site, not an implementation. X25519 is a Montgomery ladder, roughly 150\nlines with RFC 7748 vectors, and is the thing to build first. **OCapN** is an\nadapter in `kotoba-lang`, not a repository, because ADR-2608160200 already\nplaced it there.\n\nWriting a scaffold for QUIC or OPC UA and calling the gap closed was\navailable and would have been worse than leaving it open. The three\n`com-*-clean-room` repositories this ADR already names — ~190-line CRUD\nscaffolds that read like protocol implementations — are what that produces.\n\n## A fourth defect class, and a detector for it\n\n`(mapv int \"Password\")` is the code points on the JVM and a vector of\n**zeros** under ClojureScript, where a character is a one-character string.\nIt does not throw. Over one day it produced a wrong-and-plausible answer in\nthree libraries written *in* that day, twice after the author had already\nbeen bitten by it.\n\nSo it is now `scripts/verify-char-int-coercion.cljs` rather than a note. The\nfirst scan found **30 more instances across 28,008 `.cljc`/`.cljs` files**,\nfour in production source:\n\n- `kotoba-lang/koe` shipped a WAV header whose `RIFF`, `WAVE`, `fmt ` and\n  `data` magic were zero bytes — in a function whose own docstring says a\n  wrong header produces *\"not an error but a transcript of noise\"*. **Its\n  test could not catch it**: the expectation was written `[(int \\R) …]`, so\n  it degraded to `[0 0 0 0]` alongside the value and the assertion passed.\n  Both sides were wrong in the same direction.\n- `network-awai/person-isekai-hajime` summed role names to zero for every\n  role, in a ClojureScript-only repository — defeating the exact property its\n  docstring says the function exists to provide.\n\nBoth are fixed and merged. The detector reports high-confidence forms only; a\nbare `(map int x)` over a variable is not reported, because deciding whether\n`x` holds a string needs type inference it does not have and a detector that\nguesses trains people to ignore it. The lambda form counts parentheses rather\nthan matching a pattern, because `(map #(bit-and (int %) 0xff) (.getBytes s\n\\\"UTF-8\\\"))` is correct code that a regex reads identically — two real files\nhere are that shape, and both were false positives until the check stopped\nbeing a regex.\n\n## The named prerequisite, built\n\n`kotoba-lang/org-ietf-x25519` — RFC 7748 X25519. The HPKE entry said the\nblocker was that *every mention of x25519 in this workspace was a call site,\nnever an implementation*. That is no longer true, and the ledger entry now\nsays so rather than repeating a plan that has been carried out.\n\nValidated the same three ways as the others: the RFC's §5.2 and §6.1 vectors\nplus the one-round and thousand-round iteration values, all reproduced with\nBouncyCastle **before** the implementation was written; 60 differential\ncomparisons against BouncyCastle; and both runtimes, where the field is a\n`long-array` on one and a `Float64Array` on the other. Truncating the carry\ninstead of flooring it turns 65 assertions red.\n\n**Two things it records that a test could not have caught.** It is not\nconstant-time, and the README says so before it says anything else — 76 ms\nper scalar multiplication against roughly 50 µs for a native implementation,\nso where a timing side channel is in scope the platform's X25519 is the\nanswer and this is the reference it is checked against.\n\nAnd `aget` **with a boxed index falls back to reflection**. `rd` runs about\n650,000 times per scalar multiplication; without `(int i)` one X25519 took\n**24 seconds**, with it 105 ms, and with the hot multiply loop written out\n76 ms. The result was correct at every step, which is exactly why no test\nwould have found it — a 230× difference that only a measurement can see.\n\n## A failure mode the detector cannot see: a target that is a seam\n\n`kotoba-lang/org-ietf-chacha20-poly1305` — RFC 8439. Building it exposed an\nerror in this ledger that no check here would have caught.\n\nThe `@noble/ciphers` entry named `kotoba-lang/crypto` and `kotoba-lang/noise`\nas its first-party replacement. Both **exist**, so\n`substitution-target-missing` was satisfied. Neither **implements** the\ncipher: `crypto` declares an `IAEAD` protocol whose cipher is host-injected,\nand `noise` reaches ChaCha20-Poly1305 through\n`provider/{jvm.clj, noble.cljs, node.cljs}` — on ClojureScript, through\n`@noble` itself. The ledger recorded a seam as a destination, which reads as\nthough the migration were already possible.\n\nThe detector's target check compares a name against west's registry. It\ncannot tell an implementation from a protocol declaration, and adding a check\nthat tried would be guessing. **The only thing that surfaces this is somebody\nopening the target and reading it** — which is what happened here, and only\nbecause HPKE needed the cipher to actually run rather than to be namable.\n\nThe same review corrected the HPKE prerequisite: `crypto`'s `hkdf` hardcodes\na **zero salt** and its default digest is a JVM `MessageDigest`, so it is\nneither general enough for RFC 9180's labeled Extract nor portable. The\nlabeled KDF should be built on `org-nist-sha2`'s `hmac-sha256`, which is.\n\n## And a bug that survived everything except the differential run\n\nThe final `h + s` of RFC 8439 §2.5.1 is ordinary addition truncated to 128\nbits, not addition in the field. Folding a carry out of bit 130 back in as\nfive — correct *everywhere else in Poly1305* — makes the tag exactly five too\nlarge whenever the sum crosses 2^130. About one input in thirteen.\n\nIt survived **272 assertions**, the RFC's own §2.5.2 vector and the whole\nAEAD suite, because the AEAD's Poly1305 input is always padded to a multiple\nof sixteen bytes and those inputs did not cross.\n\n**The first regression test written for it did not catch it either** — eight\ntags at lengths that are not multiples of sixteen, all eight in the safe\nregion, a negative test that could not fail. That is the sixth question of\nADR-2608136000 appearing in the same afternoon it was being applied. The six\nvectors now in the suite were *searched for*: the broken version was run\nagainst BouncyCastle over 300 candidates and the 23 that disagreed were kept.\nReintroducing the wrap turns all six red, measured.\n\n## The last planned gap, closed\n\n`kotoba-lang/org-ietf-hpke` — RFC 9180 base mode. Both `:gap-planned` entries\nthis ADR opened are now resolved: HPKE is built, and the OCapN entry stands\nas an adapter decision that ADR-2608160200 already made.\n\nThe chain it took is worth recording, because each step was named before it\nwas built rather than discovered while building:\n\n    hpke-js  →  needs an AEAD and a KEM\n             →  org-ietf-chacha20-poly1305   (the AEAD; noise's was a seam)\n             →  org-ietf-x25519              (the KEM's DH; every hit was a call site)\n             →  built on org-nist-sha2       (the one hash that was already real)\n\nFour libraries, each with the next one's prerequisite stated in the ledger\nbefore work started. The ledger's value was not that it listed dependencies —\n`verify-jvm-dependency-surface` did that — but that `:prerequisite` made the\n*order* checkable.\n\n**The strongest validation available was used at the end.** Eight cases where\nBouncyCastle decrypts what this implementation sealed, rather than two\nimplementations agreeing on bytes they both computed the same wrong way.\nDropping the mode byte from the key schedule context turns 122 assertions\nred; swapping `enc` and `pkRm` in `kem_context` turns 131 red.\n\n**And the vectors' provenance is mixed, which is stated rather than\nsmoothed.** `ikmE` is RFC 9180 A.2's and `ikmR` is A.1's; both derive to the\npublic keys those sections publish and the suite asserts it, but the pairing\nis not a published combination so the ciphertexts are BouncyCastle's. An\nearlier draft of that test file claimed the whole set was A.2 verbatim. It\nwas not — the recalled `ikmR` was A.1's, and the `pkRm` assertion caught it\nbefore anything shipped. A test that asserts where its data comes from can\ncatch the author being wrong about that; one that only asserts the answer\ncannot.\n\n## Auditing the targets, after being wrong about one\n\nFinding that `@noble/ciphers` pointed at a host-injected seam raised the\nobvious question: how many of the other 57 `:first-party-exists` entries\nname a target that does not do the thing? So every distinct target was read.\n\n**One more was wrong, and it is the largest entry in the ledger.**\n`@noble/curves` named `kotoba-lang/org-ietf-ed25519`. That repository derives\nkeys and encodes them — PKCS8, SPKI, did:key, base58 — and has **no `sign`\nand no `verify`**: it delegates to the JVM's JCA and Node's `crypto.sign`.\nMeasured across the 193 repositories that declare `@noble/curves`, the\nconsumers call `sign` 193 times, `verify` 131, `getPublicKey` 138 and `Point`\n180. **None of `sign`, `verify` or `Point` exists first-party.** The entry is\nnow `:gap-planned`, with the prerequisite named: Ed25519 signing needs\nSHA-512 — `org-nist-sha2` has 224 and 256 only — and Edwards arithmetic over\nGF(2^255-19), which `org-ietf-x25519` already implements.\n\n**Five more are capability seams and six are partial**, which is not an error\nbut had been invisible. `http` and `process` declare a protocol and say so:\n\"No HTTP client is baked in\", \"host-injected IProcess\". That is the *correct*\ndesign — a capability-confined cell cannot open a socket — but it means\n\"replace `http-kit` with `kotoba-lang/http`\" is not a substitution, it is a\nsubstitution plus a host implementation. `time` is pure arithmetic with an\ninjected clock; `scheduler` a pure tick machine the host advances;\n`kotobase`'s storage backends are pluggable by design.\n\n## The field that records it\n\nEvery `:first-party-exists` entry now carries `:kind` — `:implementation`,\n`:capability-seam` or `:partial` — and a seam or partial must also say\n`:seam-because` / `:partial-because`. The detector's self-test requires all\nthree, and both requirements were shown to fail when violated.\n\nA required field cannot make anyone read the target. What it can do is make\nthe claim explicit enough that a wrong one is worth arguing with, which is\nmore than `substitution-target-missing` could offer: that check compares a\nname against west's registry, and **both wrong entries named repositories\nthat exist**.\n\nCounted after the audit: 47 implementations, 5 capability seams, 6 partial.\n\n## The gap the audit named, closed\n\nTwo libraries, in the order the ledger's `:prerequisite` field required:\n\n**SHA-512 and SHA-384** into `org-nist-sha2`. Its 88 constants were\n**derived**, not transcribed — FIPS 180-4 defines them as the fractional\nparts of the square roots of the first eight primes and the cube roots of the\nfirst eighty, so all three tables were computed to 80 significant digits and\nchecked against the published values before being written. No digit passed\nthrough anyone's memory, which is the failure a table of 88 hand-copied\n64-bit words invites. Changing one nibble of one round constant turns 812\nassertions red.\n\n**RFC 8032 sign and verify** into `org-ietf-ed25519`, over\n`org-ietf-x25519`'s GF(2^255-19) rather than a second copy of the field.\nOnly the curve is new. All four §7.1 vectors, every single-bit flip in a\nsignature and a public key rejected, `S + L` rejected, and a BouncyCastle\ndifferential **in both directions** — 12 cases where BouncyCastle verifies\nwhat this signs and 12 where this verifies what BouncyCastle signs, the\nsecond being what exercises point decoding and the square root in it.\n\nSo `@noble/curves` is `:gap-closed`, and the entry now records what it was:\n`:first-party-exists` for months, pointing at a repository that derived keys\nand encoded them while 193 call sites asked it to sign.\n\n## What the two wrong entries had in common\n\nBoth named a repository that **exists and is adjacent**. `crypto` and `noise`\nare where a caller would look for an AEAD; `org-ietf-ed25519` is where a\ncaller would look for Ed25519. Neither did the thing.\n\n`substitution-target-missing` cannot see that, and no check written here can:\ntelling an implementation from a protocol declaration is reading, not\nmatching. What the ledger can do is make the claim explicit — which is what\n`:kind` is for — and record, when a wrong one is found, how it was found. In\nboth cases it was the same way: **something needed the capability to run\nrather than to be named**, and it was not there.\n\n## Closing record — 2026-08-26\n\n### Landed on default branches\n\nTen libraries, all west-registered with `repos.edn` and `west.yml` agreeing\nand every pin equal to its repository's `main`:\n\n| repository | what |\n|---|---|\n| `org-ietf-websocket` | RFC 6455 framing and handshake |\n| `org-msgpack` | MessagePack |\n| `org-ietf-argon2` | RFC 9106, all three variants |\n| `org-modbus` | Modbus PDU, MBAP, RTU, ASCII |\n| `org-toml` | TOML v1.0.0 reader |\n| `org-ietf-x25519` | RFC 7748 |\n| `org-ietf-chacha20-poly1305` | RFC 8439 |\n| `org-ietf-hpke` | RFC 9180 base mode |\n| `org-nist-sha2` | + SHA-512 / SHA-384 |\n| `org-ietf-ed25519` | + RFC 8032 sign and verify |\n\nPlus three defect fixes merged into existing repositories — `koe` (a WAV\nheader shipping zero magic bytes), `person-isekai-hajime` (every role\nchoosing the same index), `app-scap` (six Node-builtin-named npm\ndependencies, 385 packages) — and one dependency correction in `murakumo`\n(`curves` → `@noble/curves`, which had made `npm ci` fail on every clean\ncheckout).\n\n### Verified\n\nEach library runs its suite on **both runtimes**, and each cryptographic one\nalso runs a differential against BouncyCastle 1.78.1 scoped to an alias that\nnever reaches `:deps`. Every break listed in this ADR was measured, not\nasserted. Nothing was skipped; the one suite that is slow — Ed25519 on\nClojureScript, about four minutes — is documented in its README rather than\ntrimmed.\n\n### Two detectors, and what they cannot do\n\n`verify-dependency-substitution` reports 0 unlisted coordinates, 0 missing\ntargets, 0 builtin-shadowing dependencies and 0 `:gap-open` entries across\n2,137 present checkouts.\n\n`verify-char-int-coercion` reports **26 findings, all in test files** — the\nfour in production source are fixed and merged. Closing also removed two\nfalse-positive classes it had: a docstring quoting the trap (every library\ndocumenting the bug was reported for documenting it, `koe` and\n`person-isekai-hajime` included) and the detector reporting its own fixtures.\nFour of the thirty-three findings at the start of closing were those.\n\n**Neither detector can tell an implementation from a seam.** That is the one\nlesson worth carrying: it was found twice, both times because something\nneeded a capability to *run* rather than to be named.\n\n### Resume point\n\nThe remaining work, in order of value:\n\n1. **101 rewires** — coordinates whose replacement is shipped and still\n   imported. Start with `nbb scripts/verify-dependency-substitution.cljs\n   --edn /tmp/d.edn` and filter `:substitution-available`. **Eleven of them\n   target a `:capability-seam` or `:partial`**, which means substitution plus\n   a host implementation, not substitution; `:kind` now says which.\n2. **The last `:gap-planned`** — OCapN, an adapter in `kotoba-lang/kotoba-lang`\n   over `lang/capability-semantics.edn`, not a new repository\n   (ADR-2608160200).\n3. **26 test-file coercion findings** — `org-ietf-dnssec` (9),\n   `kami-app-daw` (4), `authenticator` (4) are the concentrations. The\n   `authenticator` ones make its TOTP tests run against a zeroed RFC 6238\n   secret.\n4. The fourteen `:host-boundary` entries are **decisions, not backlog**. Each\n   names what would reopen it. Do not re-litigate them without that condition\n   being met.\n\n### Not touched\n\n`kotoba-lang/murakumo#321` is open and was left open: its author marked it a\ndraft and named a blocker that cannot be fixed automatically. No stashes, no\nworktrees and no branches from this work remain.")))
